EXPLAIN PLAN shows this query does a full table scan on a 1M-row table:
SELECT id FROM users WHERE UPPER(name) = 'ALICE';

How would you optimize?

This query is slow:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM customers WHERE status = 'inactive');

Why might it be slow, and how would you rewrite it?

A query retrieves 100k rows from a 1M-row table:
SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE status != 'deleted';

Why might an index on status not help?

This query is slow:
SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM orders ORDER BY customer_id;

A covering index on (customer_id, order_date) exists. Does it help?

A JOIN query uses 100MB memory:
SELECT o.id, c.name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id;

Large result set. How can you reduce memory?

This query returns 50k rows in 5 seconds:
SELECT * FROM large_table WHERE date >= '2024-01-01' AND status = 'active';

Indexes: (date) and (status). How would you speed this up?

EXPLAIN shows:
SELECT * FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id WHERE o.user_id IS NOT NULL;

This query is functionally equivalent to which INNER JOIN version?
